n below politicians when it  comes to a lot of public rankings so how  did you prepare a character who  intrinsically seemed unlikable but you  know you had to keep the audience  rooting for you somehow I always start  with a lot of research I start with the  script for me the script is everything I  find all the clues that I can use for  the character it helps you not play the  same character over and over again in  mini movies I read jack abramoff book  actually not even being told to or that  it was an inspiration for the script I  found out how much money was in lobbying  which was shocking to me I talked to  production about working with a lobbyist  and and they had hired someone and and I  met with him and I also I was really  important for me to meet with female  lobbyists because less than 10 percent  of lobbyists are women in DC and I  wanted to understand that dynamic so I  just like did I just googled and found  in a list of the most powerful female  obvious in DC I called their offices and  their assistants kind of thought it was  a joke or some kind of hoax and and then  I showed up one weekend and met with 11  women all of my stereotypes of what a  lobbyists were just blown out the window  seven of the eleven women had black nail  polish on which to me I was so shocked  by I thought I'd wear no makeup in this  movie and just you know be this dogged  all work person and then I realized how  these women had to present themselves in  the world it was their armor their suit  their battle cry they had to send the  energy into the room before they arrived  and that was very useful to me and also  we talked about drugs in DC and personal  lives and all that stuff so I have never  said the names of the people that I've  met with just because it was such an  honest  open conversation I wouldn't want to to  know that I blow my sources out of the  water very journalistic of you to  protect your sources John Lithgow you  had to play a senator who you know he  shifted during the course of the film  back and forth and back and forth and  yet you had to keep a core that would  also keep us interested and keep us not  entirely hating this man in fact on his  side and feeling sorry for himself at  some point how did you do that I does  this a mime it yes I can be heard all  right  I just thought of myself as a senator  and anybody in particular come to mind  not really Patrick Leahy had those  little glasses he's pretty much the only  person I didn't have time to think of  anything I was jumping off another  enormous project and virtually I got an  email from John like the last week I was  working on an 8 month long project in  England and just I think in within weeks  I was in Toronto playing this senator so  what I found fascinating with him about  him was he would be one of my senators  he would have been one of the guys I  voted for and on all on the same side as  me on all kinds of issues but you see  him capitulate to pressure which is the  most interesting and realistic thing  about Washington and about politics in  general certainly in this country is  that pressures are constantly on you and  those pressures have a lot to do with  money this of course made him a very  very interesting character to play I  read the script and it completely blew  my mind it was such a brilliant piece of  storytelling the fact that you learn  Sperling's secrets as you are learning  the secrets of the entire plot and yet  you've been watching him all this time  in the present time where all the events  of the past are brought to bear on  what's actually happening and it all  collides together at the moment  I ended the he the last thing he expects  is to be exposed in public these are  just wonderful things to hang on to for  an actor I Serb didn't need to do any  research besides which I've played three  or four senators before I was pre  researched so you have tenure yes sir I  haven't been voted out of office and  Jessica Chastain as with Oh dark thirty  you play a driven character who is  driven among men who all think they know  better so how did you strike the balance  in this one uh well in zero dark thirty  the character was so anti political  there was something almost immature  about her where she would just cut  people down and doesn't care who her  bosses and just you know she was almost  like a teenager and uh Elizabeth she's  she's very fluid with the way she can  manipulate she's almost seductive and I  needed to know it was more of a strategy  where she had to always figure out how  to get someone on her side I mean it  could be in terms of being a mentor it  could be in terms of intimidation it  could be flirting like she does with the  senator and that fundraiser there's uh  you know she has many masks this  character that she uses to her benefit  and John let's go your character in  particular finds that he has to  compromise in order to achieve the right  thing whether his selfish motives are  one thing but he believes in the  political process evidently we're seeing  this in real time in the elections right  now so so did you approach it as someone  who understands the American voter the  American voting process or was it just a  character that you plucked out of your  own ether to make the senator work well  I think that's a pretty accurate  description plucking it out of the ether  but it's so much of it was right there  in the script  he I think he just didn't he  he knew he had to go through this or he  would he would be voted out of office he  couldn't face just the power of money  the onslaught of money paid to unseat  him so we went ahead with this and just  hoped he would get through it he would  somehow discredit this woman without  doing too much damage to her and without  it going too far and then it goes  further and further and further because  nothing works to me it's it's it's  appalling to him that he has to resort  to his last couple of stratagems he  never wanted 